{
  "evidence": "TEXT_D: Free personal care for the elderly; restore free dental and eye checks; patient guarantees, advocates and no‑fault compensation; greener homes and cleaner air; end mixed wards; carbon tax, 10% renewables, phase out nuclear; recycling to 60%, brownfield first, protect green spaces, flood resilience; 6,000 more police plus independent police complaints, evidence‑led stop and search; protect jury trial/right to silence; judicial warrants and no ID cards; free off‑peak buses for pensioners/disabled. TEXT_E: 20,000 extra nurses and 10,000 extra doctors; modernise the NHS for faster free treatment; support for children and families and better cash/services for pensioners; 6,000 extra police and criminal justice reform to catch, punish and rehabilitate; Britain strong in the world, engage and lead in Europe; fight climate change and global poverty; maintain stability with low inflation and sound public finances.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts speak strongly to Care, but TEXT_D adds more concrete, person‑centred health protections and everyday alleviation of harm (free personal care, dental/eye checks, patient advocates) alongside mobility support for vulnerable groups. With Sanctity the second‑highest priority, TEXT_D’s extensive ‘clean/green’ and purity‑adjacent agenda (cleaner air, recycling, brownfield first, ending mixed wards) is far more salient than TEXT_E’s brief climate references, while both adequately acknowledge Authority, making TEXT_D a closer fit overall.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_D"
}
